28. Text to speech: what players near you say in chat, spoken (H9Tts, cl_h9_tts, cl_h9_tts_volume, _h9tts)

   A server with h9_texttospeech 1 (h9_tts.amxx; docs\TEXT_TO_SPEECH.txt) sends each player near a speaker the H9Tts user message:
   byte slot, byte voice (0 man, 1 woman, 2 Mech, 3 bot), byte volume 0 to 100 (from the distance), byte flags (bit 0, a gas mask
   muffles the voice), string words. cl_dll\h9_tts.cpp hooks it in CHud::Init (H9Tts_Init, after H9Vgui_Init in hud.cpp) and checks
   it against its own limits whatever the message says: a line is cleaned and cut to 160 bytes, at most 8 lines a second are read,
   no speaker's lines come closer than 0.3 s; whom a line is sent to is the server's say (the speaker too while h9_tts_self is 1).
   cl_h9_tts (1, saved) is 0 for silence;
   cl_h9_tts_volume (1, saved, 0 to 2) scales the voices. The game says it can speak with the userinfo key _h9tts, as it says it
   draws H9 VGUI with _h9vgui: "1" while cl_h9_tts is not 0 and the machine has a voice, else "0"; a game that does not say "1" is
   sent nothing. HUD_Shutdown (input.cpp) calls H9Tts_Shutdown, which stops the speech thread.
   VOICES TO PICK (13:45): cl_h9_tts_voice_man, _woman, _mech and _bot (saved, 0) hold a place, from 1, in the list cl_h9_tts_voices prints of the
   voices installed (SAPI's on Windows, listed once by the worker thread; the browser's in the web client); 0 leaves the choice to the game, and a Mech
   or a bot with no pick of its own speaks in the man's. cl_h9_tts_test <man|woman|mech|bot> [muffled] <words> speaks a line in that voice with the
   picks in force (speaker 255). cl_h9_tts_robot (saved, 1) puts a Mech's and a bot's voice through a ring modulator on Windows (a 38 Hz and a 62 Hz
   hum, 65 percent of the line; the browser has no audio to filter, so there it keeps its pitch and rate). H9TtsPlat_Speak takes the pick and the robot
   flag; H9TtsPlat_VoiceCount and H9TtsPlat_VoiceInfo list the voices. ts\ttsactors.cfg (exec ttsactors.cfg) lists them and explains the picks.

   The speaking is the platform's, behind h9_tts_platform.h. Windows (h9_tts_win.cpp, which includes none of the client's headers):
   one worker thread with its own COM apartment, so the render thread only queues a line. SAPI renders it to memory through an
   ISpStream (22050 Hz, 16 bit, mono) with SPF_IS_NOT_XML, so nothing a player types is taken for markup; the voice is the first
   installed one whose Gender is Male or Female (English US, then UK, then any); a Mech is resampled to 0.80 of the speed (slower and
   lower), a bot to 1.22; a gas mask runs a low-pass biquad (1200 Hz, with 1.1 gain back) over the samples; the distance volume is
   multiplied into the samples (a volume of 1 leaves 4 dB of headroom); waveOut plays them. At most 3 voices play together, a
   speaker's newer line stops his older one, 8 lines wait at most. The build links ole32 and winmm (CMakeLists.txt) and not sapi.lib: the
   wave-format GUID is defined in h9_tts_win.cpp and SAPI is reached through COM, so client.dll has no load-time dependency on
   sapi.dll and a machine without SAPI says _h9tts 0. The web client (h9_tts_web.cpp, compiled when __EMSCRIPTEN__ is defined) calls
   the page's speechSynthesis through one function installed with emscripten_run_script (EM_JS is not resolved in a side module,
   as freevgui's font.cpp notes): a voice by name for a man or a woman, a queue of three spoken one at a time, a Mech at pitch 0.5
   and rate 0.8, a bot at 1.7 and 1.25, a gas mask approximated by 0.7 of the volume, 0.9 of the pitch and 0.95 of the rate. Any
   other platform compiles to a no-op. The client.dll this replaced is kept as client.dll.bak_pretts in reTS\binaries\ts\cl_dlls\.

   VOICES, MASK AND EARS (2026-11-09, evening; replaces the voice choice, the 1200 Hz low-pass and the mono output described above). The
   H9Tts message has a tail after the words: byte variant (0 none, 1 to 99), char depth (-10 to 10), char pan (-100 his voice in the left ear to 100); the
   server (h9_tts.amxx) fills it from the speaker's h9_ttsvoice and from where he is in the listener's view, and the client reads it only when the message is
   long enough for it. A message with slot 0 and flag bit 1 is the server asking this game for its picks: the game then sends the server command
   "h9_ttsvoice <cl_h9_tts_myvoice> <cl_h9_tts_mydepth>" (not through the userinfo, which is full), and again whenever either cvar changes
   (CHudH9Tts::Think; VidInit forgets that it has been asked, so each map starts over). New client cvars, all saved: cl_h9_tts_myvoice (0 to 99, 0: each
   listener's game chooses), cl_h9_tts_mydepth (-10 to 10), cl_h9_tts_muffle (0 to 2, 1), cl_h9_tts_pan (1), cl_h9_tts_maxvoices (1 to 3, 2),
   cl_h9_tts_individual (1: a speaker who asked for no voice gets one, and a small step off its pitch, from his slot). H9TtsPlat_Speak takes an
   H9TtsLine now. Windows: the default voice is the best listed one of its gender (a Natural or Neural voice ahead of a plain one, a voice without
   "Desktop" ahead of one with, English of the US, then the UK, ahead of other tongues) and variant n is the n-th of those, wrapping round (a listener's own
   pick still wins); a speaker's depth resamples by 1.025 for each step on top of the kind's speed; the gas mask is two low-passes (a fourth-order
   Butterworth at 1800 Hz / (1 + cl_h9_tts_muffle), 900 Hz by default) and a +4 dB resonance at 700 Hz, then scaled back to 85 percent of the RMS the
   line had, so the mask is heard as dullness and not as distance; the output is stereo (waveOut, 2 channels) with a constant-power pan, 0.85 of the way
   into one ear at most; when as many voices as cl_h9_tts_maxvoices are playing, a line louder than the quietest by a fifth takes its place and a quieter one
   is not spoken. The browser: the n-th voice (a Natural or Neural one ahead), the pitch x (1 + 0.05 for each step of depth), the gas mask scaled by
   cl_h9_tts_muffle; no pan, one voice at a time. The client.dll this replaced is kept as client.dll.bak_pretts2 in reTS\binaries\ts\cl_dlls\.

   MIXED VOICES AND GLOBAL SPEECH (2026-11-10; replaces the playback of the evening batch above: one waveOut device for each voice, one thread that both rendered and
   played, and the rule that a louder line takes the quietest voice's place and a quieter one is not spoken). cl_dll\h9_tts_mix.h (new, no platform code, listed in
   cl_dll\CMakeLists.txt) is the mixer: voices of different speakers play together, up to cl_h9_tts_maxvoices (now 1 to 8, default 4; was 1 to 3, default 2), summed
   and passed through a soft limiter (exact below 26000, never past full scale), each fading in and out over 5 ms. A line that arrives when that many play WAITS for a
   voice (4 lines, none longer than 8 seconds after it arrived; a full list lets the quietest go, the oldest of equals) and no playing line is cut off; a speaker's
   lines follow one another (a newer line of his replaces his waiting one). h9_tts_win.cpp has two threads now: the speech thread (COM MTA; SAPI renders, the samples
   are resampled, robot-filtered and masked, then handed to the mixer; 8 lines wait for it, the quietest dropped, and a line older than 8 seconds or overtaken by a
   Stop is not spoken) and an audio thread that owns ONE waveOut device (22050 Hz stereo, 4 blocks of 40 ms, CALLBACK_EVENT; opened for the first line, let go of after
   2.5 seconds of silence, tried again after 1 second when it will not open). The volume and the constant-power pan are multiplied in by the mixer; a shaped line is
   scaled to a peak of 32000 instead of clipping. H9Tts flags bit 2 (value 4) marks a global line (the server's h9_tts_global 1: sent to everyone, volume 100, pan 0):
   the client plays it from no direction, and a client that does not know the bit plays it as a full-volume line from the middle. cl_h9_tts 0 now stops what plays and
   what waits (H9TtsPlat_Stop). The browser (h9_tts_web.cpp) cannot overlap voices: its queue is 4 lines and 8 seconds, the quietest goes, a speaker's newer line
   replaces his waiting one and no longer cancels the one being spoken, the utterance is kept referenced until it ends, and cl_h9_tts 0 cancels. A saved
   cl_h9_tts_maxvoices keeps its value.

